\title{The heat accounting cost more than the compiled blocks won back}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{truffle}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-truffle/NOTES-T3.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [rep-truffle-T3](rep-truffle-T3), section 3; [the profiling session](ses-JRSD). reachability, one worker, quiet windows, the tier as its first owner left it, whose governor promoted a block by measured time.}
\ul{
\li{Counting every entry of a block costs 2.4 percent on the JVM and 5.0 percent in the native image against the stepper alone.}
\li{Timing on top, even at one sampled entry in 4,096, costs 11 percent on the JVM and 8.5 percent in the image, because a sampled entry times every block entered beneath it.}
\li{Compiled blocks win back about that much and no more: against "tier attached, nothing promoted" they gain 4.7 percent on the JVM and 1.7 percent in the image. Net against the stepper: -2.5 percent to parity on the JVM, +3.3 percent in the image, +2 to +4 percent on the Cedar slice.}
}
\p{The final policy never switches the heat accounting of the machine on: the tier counts, per machine, the activations it does not take. A governor that rents by measured time has to earn its own meter first, and here it could not.}
\p{See also: [the Truffle tier](design-truffle-tier).}
